# Containers Calling Workers Bindings via Outbound Handlers

Containers can access Workers bindings (R2, D1, KV, Durable Objects) through outbound handlers that run in the Workers runtime outside the container sandbox.

## How It Works

Outbound handlers intercept HTTP requests and execute in the Workers environment, giving them access to all bindings. The documentation explains: "Outbound handlers are programmable egress proxies that run on the same machine as the container. They have access to all Workers bindings."

## Implementation Pattern

Use `outboundByHost` to map specific domains to handler functions:

```js
MyContainer.outboundByHost = {
  "my.worker": async (request, env, ctx) => {
    // Access env.BINDING_NAME here
    return await someWorkersFunction(request.body);
  },
};
```

The container makes plain HTTP requests to hostnames you define, while handlers execute binding operations securely outside the sandbox.

## Security Benefits

This architecture prevents containers from directly accessing credentials. Handlers can inject authentication headers or perform binding operations without exposing secrets to untrusted container code—useful for agentic workloads where container code cannot be fully trusted.

